{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Registered the necessary gap the asserted claim depends on, after re-deriving the propose-time guard's premises more carefully rather than letting the asserted claim outrun its evidence. The receipts do establish the consequence chain - a joint-enter change that alters no membership, no leave-joint entry anywhere in the applied range, in-order application by three nodes, and the Changer's rejection that applyConfChange turns into a process-fatal panic - and the deterministic case shows the guard admitting a second joint-enter change when pendingConfIndex and applied both lag a committed joint change while the tracker is not joint. What no receipt shows is how a leader reaches exactly that triple in a running cluster: becomeLeader resets pendingConfIndex to the last index, alreadyPending is true whenever pendingConfIndex exceeds applied, and a leader that has applied the committed joint change holds a joint tracker, so the admitting state requires a leader whose log grew after it took office while its applied index stayed below the older pendingConfIndex - a specific, testable history, but not one the sweep's receipts exhibit. The gap records both live possibilities: a permitted history in a live cluster (for example the joint change committed by a predecessor while the node took office before that entry existed) or a harness effect such as a restart re-initialising pendingConfIndex and applied while the log already held the change. I left the defect claim asserted on what the receipts do show rather than weakening it back to unresolved without new evidence, or extending it to a trigger I cannot yet source - the next step is to instrument the sweep to print, at the moment the second joint change is appended, the proposer's pendingConfIndex, applied index and tracker jointness, which would either name the live history or show that the sweep's occurrence came from its own bookkeeping.\"}"}}
